I don't think I've ever killed the Wizard so few times, not certain of
it though.  Quasits were my most-killed monster, unsurprisingly, gnomes
were #2, vampire lords (of all things) seemed to be #3, although I have
the feeling I missed some entries scanning the list (soldiers and ochre
jellies - I can't imagine I killed fewer than 40 of each).

I genocided 4 types (all L).
I used 7 wishes, no artifacts.

I had all the intrinsics you would expect except teleportitis, and in addition
had teleport control and was the Hand of Elbereth.

I ended up with 3,983,721 points, some from Excalibur, the Bell (not sure
why I still had it, I dropped the Book and the Candelabrum), the Magic
Mirror of Merlin, and my 3 AoLS (and presumably also from the Angel
of Lugh, who ascended with me).

This my third-lowest-scoring ascension, and also my third-slowest -
only my very first two ascensions were slower (76,752 and 69,265 turns).
My per-turn score of 63.45 is my second-worst, only my first ascension
was lower.  

The game was fairly standard, I think, without much of note, other than
minor stupid stuff caused by my rustiness or carelessness.  I was careful
to let my horse kill a lot and bulk up, and he was handy to have around for
a while.  I would have had him around longer except I didn't think to lock
him up in Minetown, and he tried to take on the priestess.  Fortunately
I didn't need him to protect me by that point (riding was nice, but not
critical).

I spent a fair amount of time trudging back and forth through the levels
for various reasons, which no doubt accounts for the large number of turns.
I went back to Minetown several times to buy protection and also to price-ID
scrolls, as the only shop in the game that would buy them (a bookstore)
was there (there were not a lot of shops - outside Minetown there was an
armor shop on level 5 or so and then a potion shop on level 20 and
a hardware store on level 23).  I managed to get hit by mind flayers a few 
times in the Castle which necessitated an additional price-ID trip.

I did Sokoban fairly early and got an AoR.  Luckily a throne on a nearby
level gave me a wish, which I used for a blessed BoH.  I managed to pick
up decent kit from one place or another (horse-killed dwarves, etc.),
including +2 dwarvish mithril, a CoP, GoP (and the GoD, but I wore the
GoP), and the HoB.  I dipped for Excalibur as soon as I could.  I don't
remember where I got the silver saber - probably in Ludious, but it might have
been earlier.

The Minetown altar was not Lugh's, but I found two of his on the middle
of the dungeon on adjacent levels.  A few quick sacrifices produced
Snickersnee.  Since this is demonstrably worse than Excalibur, I was
underwhelmed.  However, apparently Lugh felt he had done his job for the
day, since no matter how much more I sacrificed, I got nothing more
from him.  I went through those two levels numerous times and must have
done at least 80 sacrifices, taking all the SoCM, WoCM, BoT, and wandering
monsters into account - no more gifts.  I was a bit irked.  I was also
irked when I was crowned the second time I prayed for holy water,
but it wasn't actually a problem (and it did give me fire resistance,
which I hadn't managed to get).

I cleaned out Ludious as soon as I found it (with reflection, not
difficult) and maxed out my protection immediately afterwards.  I
proceeded down to Medusa, then went back up to do the Quest, not
having any means of crossing water.  The Quest was pretty trivial -
I did lose my CoP and GoP to Ixoth's spells, but I wasn't planning
to have either in my ascension kit anyway, so not too big a deal.
Somewhere around there I got levitation boots and did Medusa and
continued downwards (I picked up a ring shortly after that).

In the Castle I decided to go in the back way, which proved to be a
mistake as a master lich decided to pop out to bug me when I was
nearly on the opposite side. To kill him I would have had to go back
to the stairs, which in theory I could have managed, but it would
have been tough.  As I had a blessed magic lamp in my bag, I wished
up two blessed SoG and removed the problem.  After that the Castle
was easy.

After clearing the Castle I pondered the armor situation.  I had
already decided to do without a shield.  Partly this was because
I mistakenly thought that it would inhibit spellcasting, but I
also wanted to #twoweapon.  I also wanted to wear my GoD, HoB, and the
CoD I found in the Castle storeroom, as I've always been fond of
displacement.  And of course I needed speed boots.  The body armor
was the only problem, as I had not managed to get silver scales
(I only ever saw one silver dragon).  I would have taken grey as
an alternative - the Mirror gives MR, but a backup is not a terrible
idea in case Rodney steals it.  However, no grey scales either.
By the time I cleared the Castle I would have taken yellow, just
for the sake of having an extra resistance, but although I thought
I had killed a yellow scale-leaving dragon while dealing with
the lich, I couldn't find any scales.  

I used another magic lamp I found in the Castle to get speed boots
and postponed the decision to bone up on my magic.  I had read
spellbooks as I found them, mostly, but there were a lot in Minetown
I hadn't read, so I trekked back there AGAIN to do so.  I ended
up with force bolt, fireball, magic missile, healing, cure sickness,
and a bunch of stuff I would never be able to cast.

Still no silver, grey, or yellow dragons.  So I gave up.  I used another
magic lamp I found in the Castle for speed boots, then used the WoW
for SoC, SDSM, and a magic marker. I used the marker to write SoEA,
but naturally (the way my luck was going), it ran dry after 2.  I used
such SoEA as I had, considered recharging the marker, and decided
to forget about it until I thought I needed better AC.  In the event
I never did (I did read one more SoEA I found in Gehennom).  Of course
I still had my +3 ring of protection - I found this pretty early
on and rarely took it off.

Of course, in the Valley of the Dead I found a blessed pair of speed
boots.  I also found my missing yellow dragon scales - something had
picked them up and then fell through a trap door.  Oh well.

Gehennom was fairly easy.  I had a fair number of hitpoints going
in (175 or so), as I had done some alchemy and gotten some extra 
PoGL, plus found wraith corpses here and there.  I also made a bunch
of PoFL, but kept most around for emergencies.  To avoid getting
burdened I just chugged any sort of healing potion I found in Gehennom,
though (and I got a few more wraiths).  

I hadn't done much spellcasting since 3.2.2 (and I didn't focus on
it then), so I was eager to try my shiny new attack spells.  They
worked pretty well, especially with the Mirror's boost.  I didn't
cast anything else except remove curse, which I used quite a few
times.  I made it to Skilled in attack spells and Basic in clerical
(oddly the game showed that I could still advance in attack).

Asmodeus escaped and I had to whack him on the way back up.  Baalzebub
and Juiblex were pushovers as usual.  Orcus for some reason never used
his WoD, which I was grateful for (although I never got to use it myself,
it got blown up somewhere on the planes).  

Given that so many Gehennom nasties are immune to fireballs, I really
wanted the other area-effect spell, cone of cold.  No such luck.  In
Vlad's tower I found a bunch of spellbooks - still no luck.  I hadn't
polymorphed anything yet, but since I wasn't actually trying to keep
the conduct I used up two PoPoly and a bunch of WoP charges on them -
no cone of cold.  I finally gave up and just wished for one (my 7th
and last wish) before going into the Tower.

Rodney was easier than I'd ever seen him, so much so I wondered if
he'd been downgraded in 3.4.  He only showed up once before the
Plane of Earth, and was easy to kill every time (two or three 
whacks, no Double Trouble, no stealing).  

The Sanctum was also easy, I just went as fast as possible in and out,
bot bothering to pick up stuff.  

Continuing this theme, the planes were also very easy.  I mean, earth
and fire always are, but even air was no problem, as I had a RoL
and RoFA.  I got swallowed four or five times, but I don't think 
I got knocked down below 170 HP or so before killing the elemental
any of those times.  I had exactly four SoGD, so I just used those
to find the portals.

My one last stupid move (I had made a few others earlier, such as
the getting hit by mind flayers - there were other, less drastic ones)
was accidentally stepping out of the air bubble on Water for one
turn.  Not a major problem except that my 13 diluted PoFH turned
to water - I still had 2-3 left, but I wanted more.  However,
nothing to be done about it.

Finally, the Astral Plane was ALSO about the easiest I've ever
had it.  I went up the center to start, which turned out to
contain Famine.  I managed to consistently miss him with my
remaining WoD, but he went down pretty easily to swords when 
I gave up on the wand.  He did get to me once, but I still had
a few rations in the bag (and a horn of plenty, actually), so 
no problem.  I was able to get to the altar in record time (for
me, anyway), hardly anything in the way, and it was the correct
one.  I waited a few turns for the Angel to catch up, and that
was it.


